# LugalOS Documentation

Welcome to the documentation for LugalOS, a bare-metal, capability-isolated RISC-V microkernel operating system with Plan 9 IPC, exact Lisp algebra, on-device compilation, and horizontal tiling window ribbons.

## Documentation Structure

The documentation is organized into three comprehensive manuals, an Architecture Decision Record (ADR) repository, and a generated on-device help system:

```
doc/
|-- user/             User Manual: Day-to-day operation, shell, and applications
|-- admin/            Administrator Manual: Hardware presets, flashing, and networking
|-- dev/              System Programmer Manual: C & Lisp APIs, kernel internals, and testing
|-- history/          Architecture Decision Records (ADRs)
\-- assets/           Hardware photographs, diagrams, and terminal screenshots
```

* About LugalOS: High-level architecture overview, hardware targets, on-device tools, and system concepts.

## 1. User Manual

Guides for operating LugalOS interactively via terminals and serial consoles:

* Shell & Commands: Interactive lsh shell, file manipulation, pipeline & redirection model, procfs/devfs navigation, job control.
* Text Editors: Full-screen Emacs editor (e), teletype line editor (ed), keybindings, and buffer management.
* Window Ribbon & Terminal: Horizontal tiling window manager, touch gestures (tap, swipe), keyboard navigation (Cmd+Left/Right), split canvases, screenshots.
* Chess Computer: Operating the chess engine on RP2350 and display terminals, Lazy SMP dual-core search, TM1638 keypad controls, and game history.
* Computer Algebra System (CAS): Exact symbolic mathematics, canonical polynomial simplification, equation solving, series expansion, and plotting.
* Environmental Sensors & Telemetry: Autonomous sensor node operation, I2C environmental telemetry (BME280/BMP280), and MQTT 3.1.1 publishing.
* Z-Machine & Interactive Fiction: Playing Infocom and v3 stories, split-screen canvas auto-mapper, and save slot persistence.

## 2. Administrator Manual

Guides for hardware provisioning, flashing, networking, and storage:

* Hardware Presets Matrix: Comparison of all supported silicon targets (RP2350, ESP32-P4, ESP32-C6, QEMU RV32/RV64).
* Firmware Flashing Guide: Step-by-step flashing procedures via picotool, p4flash.py, and c6flash.py.
* Consoles & Serial Connections: Dynamic TTY port mapping, CDC-ACM dual ports, CH343P/CP2102 baud rates (921600 on ESP32-P4), and modem control (DTR/RTS).
* Networking & Plan 9 Services: 9P filesystem protocol, p9sync.py synchronization, fuse-p9 host mounts, SLIP demultiplexing, and Wi-Fi / Ethernet setups.
* Identity & Security Records: Board provisioning, credential storage (idstore), Wi-Fi PSK derivation, and mutual authentication.
* Storage & Filesystems: MicroSD FAT32 formatting, flashfs ROM disks, and RAM disks.
* Updating Nodes over 9P: A/B partition updates, trial booting, and rollback.
* Target Hardware & Board Reference Guides: Operational guides for ESP32-P4-LCD-7B, M5Stack Tab5, ESP32-C6, and RP2350.

## 3. System Programmer Manual

Guides for developing user-space applications and extending the LugalOS kernel:

### Part A: On-Device Application Development
* C Programming on LugalOS: On-device compilation with chibicc, freestanding C subset (lugal.h), system call ABI, and ELF execution under PMP domains.
* Lisp Scripting & Extensions: LugalOS Scheme/Lisp dialect, zero-SRAM builtins in Flash .rodata, standard library modules, macros, and CAS extensions.

### Part B: Kernel & Driver Architecture
* Microkernel Architecture: Capability-style memory domains (PMP on NOMMU, Sv39 page tables on MMU), copy-always message channels (chan_t), and U-mode task isolation.
* Scheduler & SMP Multicore: Priority tiers, preemptive timer scheduling, cross-hart scheduler lock hand-off, and atomic primitives (spinlockt, ylockt).
* Tokenized Kernel Logging: High-efficiency dictionary logging, .rodata format string pointers, varargs capturing in klogemittok(), and deferred expansion via klogd.
* Driver Framework: Confined U-mode driver tasks, hardware register window grants, IRQ dispatch, and fault containment.
* Board Porting Guide: Adding new SoCs and boards, linker layouts, clock trees, stage 2 bootloaders, and XIP flash mapping.

### Part C: Testing Framework & QA
* Testing Guide: Unit tests, ASan/UBSan host harnesses, automated QEMU regression runner (tests/runner.py), and hardware-in-the-loop silicon suites (tests/hw/).

### Part D: Wire & Interconnect Protocols
* LugalOS Link Protocol (LLP): Point-to-point link protocol multiplexing 9P filesystem calls and raw 802.3 Ethernet frames between coprocessors with credit-based flow control.

## 4. Architecture Decision Records (ADRs)

Formal records documenting critical architectural choices, trade-offs, and historical rationale:

* ADR 001: Zero Floating-Point in Kernel / M-Mode
* ADR 002: Scheduler Lock Hand-Off Across Context Switches
* ADR 003: Tokenized Dictionary Logging in Kernel Ring
* ADR 004: Copy-Always Message Channels for IPC
* ADR 005: Zero SRAM Builtins in Flash .rodata

## 5. On-Device Help System

All chapters from the User and Administrator manuals are automatically compiled by tools/gendocs.py into 78-column plain-text help files stored on device storage at /sd0/help/ (and /flash0/help/).

On a running LugalOS terminal or serial console:
* Type help for an overview of shell commands.
* Type help  or man  to read interactive help with terminal paging and single-key [1..9] link jumps.

